  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nRAP analysis indicates a cold front dropping into southeast \nWisconsin early this afternoon, with surface high pressure \ncentered over the northern Plains steadily building eastward \ntoward the western Great Lakes in its wake. West winds behind the\ndeparting cold front have become gusty early this afternoon, with\nwidespread gusts between 25 and 30 mph and occasional peak gusts \nreaching 35 mph across north-central Wisconsin. A Small Craft \nAdvisory remains in effect for the Bay of Green Bay and Lake \nMichigan nearshore waters north of Sturgeon Bay through 7 PM CDT \ndue to these gusty winds and building wave action. \n\nGusty Winds Today and Fog Potential Tonight...\nWind gusts will subside rapidly early this evening as boundary \nlayer mixing wanes and surface high pressure slides into the \nstate. A dry airmass accompanying the surface high, combined with \nmodest boundary layer winds, will limit fog formation overnight \ndespite clear skies and modest surface winds. Tranquil conditions\nwill prevail through Thursday under high pressure, with scattered\nfair- weather cumulus developing late Thursday morning with bases\nbetween 4000 and 5000 feet. High temperatures on Thursday will \nremain near seasonal norms in the 70s.\n\nThunderstorm Potential Friday Night and Saturday...\nThe weather pattern becomes more active heading into the end of \nthe work week as shortwave energy lifts northeast across the \nnorthern Plains into south-central Canada. This shortwave will \npush a cold front across Wisconsin late Friday night through \nSaturday morning. Ahead of the boundary, a narrow moisture channel\nwill advect into the region, pushing precipitable water values up\nto 140 to 150 percent of normal. Despite respectable synoptic \ndynamics and moisture, the risk for organized severe weather \nappears low. Model sounding profiles display a significant capping\ninversion centered around 800 mb, which should limit surface-\nbased convection. While elevated instability of 600 to 800 J/kg \nexists above the cap, instability diminishes significantly with \neastward extent late Friday night into Saturday morning. \nConsequently, scattered thunderstorms and rain showers are \nexpected along and ahead of the front, but severe weather is not \nanticipated.\n\nThunderstorm and Heavy Rainfall Potential Sunday and Monday... \nForecast attention turns to the Sunday into Monday timeframe as \nthe frontal boundary returns north as a warm front. A wave of \nsurface low pressure is forecast to track northeast along the \nfront, with the latest guidance showing the low passing across \nnorthwest Wisconsin. Guidance spread remains rather large \nregarding the precise surface low track and timing, leading to low\nconfidence in the exact details. However, dynamic forcing aloft \ncombined with excessive moisture transport bringing precipitable \nwater values approaching 200 percent of normal creates a signal \nfor heavy rainfall. The Extreme Forecast Index highlights \nWisconsin for potential heavy rain, with probabilities for greater\nthan 1 inch of rainfall reaching 40 to 50 percent over north- \ncentral Wisconsin. This widespread precipitation would offer \nbeneficial moisture to drought-stricken areas across far northern \nWisconsin. Should the low track across northwest Wisconsin as \nadvertised, portions of the forecast area could enter the warm \nsector, allowing Most Unstable CAPE to reach 1000 to 1500 J/kg if \nsufficient surface heating occurs. This potential setup would \nfavor a risk for severe thunderstorms, though overall confidence \nremains low given the extended timeframe.",
